WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.180
Since he made his debut in 1988, Venom has
become one of Spider-Man’s deadliest and

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:06.580
most well-known foes.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:10.400
So well-known, in fact, that you’re probably
already familiar with most of the facts about

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.429
his origin.

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:15.329
From the alien costume that Spidey picked
up in Secret Wars to his defeat by a church

00:00:15.329 --> 00:00:19.780
bell and even his appearance in the classic
Maximum Carnage video game, you might think

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:20.780
you know it all.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:24.249
Dig a little deeper, though, and you might
find a few facts that you didn’t know about

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:25.249
Venom.

00:00:25.249 --> 00:00:27.300
Venom: A David Goyer Film

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.349
While Tom Hardy will be taking on the role
of Venom in the character's first major solo

00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:35.000
film, it's hardly the first time someone has
tried to bring Venom to the big screen for

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.000
his own movie.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.359
There were plans for a spinoff series after
Eddie Brock made his appearance as one of

00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:44.170
the villains in 2007's Spider-Man 3, but ten
years earlier, New Line Cinema was making

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.310
a serious effort to make Venom: The Motion
Picture happen.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.269
While it never happened, it boasted a screenplay
by David S. Goyer, and was rumored to have

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:53.320
Dolph Lundgren in talks to star.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.989
Not a bad choice when you consider how often
artists of the '90s drew Eddie Brock to look

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:59.739
like an even more jacked version of Ivan Drago.

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:04.260
Goyer would eventually break through into
superhero films as the writer of 1998's Blade

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.630
and its sequels, and would go on to provide
the scripts for Batman Begins, Man of Steel

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:10.540
and Dawn of Justice.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:11.910
What could have been.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.570
Truth in Journalism

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.400
If big-budget superhero movies aren't your
thing, you don't need to wait to see an interesting

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.330
live-action take on Venom thanks to Joe Lynch's
Truth in Journalism.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:27.020
Released as a fan film in 2013, the documentary-style
movie follows disgraced photojournalist "Eddie

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.980
B." as he makes his way through the streets
of New York, finding — and occasionally

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.580
manufacturing — salacious news stories.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.060
Needless to say, it turns out that Eddie's
hiding an even bigger secret.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.350
Spoiler warning: it’s a weird symbiote monster
from space who eats people.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.910
Even though most viewers know exactly who
“Eddie B.” is from the first frame, the

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:49.030
big reveal of Venom at the end is well-done
and tense, with Ryan Kwanten, of True Blood

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:53.380
fame, doing a great job of playing Brock as
an amoral opportunist, If you've never seen

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:57.100
it, it's well worth 20 minutes of your time
— and like all Marvel movies, make sure

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.500
to stick around after the credits.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.140
Venom vs. No. 7

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:05.590
In the 1990s, Venom was easily one of Marvel's
single most popular characters, for a pretty

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:06.810
obvious reason.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:11.000
He was basically Frank Castle plus Peter Parker,
with a mouth full of teeth so long and sharp

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.290
that even Wolverine thought it was a bit much.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:18.541
He was so popular, in fact, that from 1993
to 1998, there was a new issue of Venom on

00:02:18.541 --> 00:02:21.239
the stands every single month without missing
a beat.

00:02:21.239 --> 00:02:24.510
Those issues just never managed to get past
number 6.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:29.209
Rather than being a single comic that ran
from #1 to #60, Venom's ongoing adventures

00:02:29.209 --> 00:02:34.150
were broken out into a staggering 17 different
series that ran back to back to back to back

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:36.040
to back to — well, you get the idea.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.709
It started in February of '93 with Venom:
Lethal Protector, and ran through Separation

00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:45.340
Anxiety, Sinner Takes All, License to Kill,
and a dozen other books, before finally wrapping

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.819
up with the appropriately named Venom: The
Finale in January of 1998.

00:02:49.819 --> 00:02:53.560
As for why they didn't just keep Lethal Protector
going, well, that's simple.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:57.060
Everyone knows that a #1 issue is more valuable
for collectors, right?

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.980
And what's better than one #1?

00:02:58.980 --> 00:02:59.980
Seventeen #1s!

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.510
Wait, what do you mean that's not how supply
and demand works?

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:04.920
The thin line between love and hate

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:09.340
It was bad enough when Peter Parker discovered
that his black costume was a living creature

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.069
hell-bent on controlling his body, but it
got even worse when the symbiote joined up

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:17.370
with Eddie Brock, whose hatred of Parker only
amplified the alien’s resentment after being

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.370
rejected.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:22.040
It doesn't take a lot of analysis to see the
subtext in that relationship: the rejection,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:26.290
the jealousy, the new partner picked up on
the rebound — for all intents and purposes,

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.379
the symbiote is reacting like a scorned lover.

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:31.959
It isn’t just subtext, it’s the whole
point of one of the greatest Venom stories

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:32.959
of all time.

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:38.200
In Amazing Spider-Man #317, Eddie Brock challenges
Peter to a one-on-one fight to the death.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:43.080
Alone and completely outmatched in terms of
strength, Peter makes the desperate move of...

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.010
stripping off his costume and seducing the
Venom suit with the proportionate sexiness

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.010
of a spider.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:52.050
If the subtext had been difficult to see before,
then the sight of Peter Parker standing on

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:56.109
a beach in tiny underpants offering up his
body put an exclamation point on the whole

00:03:56.109 --> 00:03:57.109
thing.

00:03:57.109 --> 00:04:00.109
It actually worked, too, but the symbiote's
relationship with Eddie turned out to be too

00:04:00.109 --> 00:04:03.829
deep to drop all at once, leaving him passed
out on the beach in his underpants covered

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:05.639
in a viscous ooze.

00:04:05.639 --> 00:04:07.879
And who hasn’t been there after a bad breakup?

00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:09.439
Superman smackdown

00:04:09.439 --> 00:04:13.519
While he's come pretty close on several occasions,
Venom has never been able to truly defeat

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.099
Spider-Man once and for all.

00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:19.040
He did, however, beat the living heck out
of Superman, so he's got that going for him.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.250
It happened in a follow-up to the world-colliding
DC vs. Marvel crossover.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.070
To his credit, Superman manages to hold his
own, but you'd think that a guy who can shoot

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:31.110
heat lasers out of his eyes and make a sonic
boom by clapping his hands would probably

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:35.880
have a pretty easy time fighting someone whose
major weaknesses are loud noises and fire.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.250
And yet, once Venom flies into a rage, he
not only goes toe-to-toe with the Last Son

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:44.690
of Krypton, he also beats up Superman and
Spider-Man at the same time.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:48.001
The only reason the good guys are able to
pull off the win is the intervention of a

00:04:48.001 --> 00:04:53.070
third character, Access, an obscure goofball
who's jointly owned by Marvel and DC... and

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:57.720
apparently has the deus ex machina powers
of both companies rolled into one.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.410
An extra sting in the tail

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:04.220
If you're familiar with Mac Gargan, it's probably
from his original villainous identity: the

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.220
Scorpion.

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.890
Introduced in 1964, Gargan was a crooked private
eye hired by J. Jonah Jameson to dig up dirt

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:11.670
on everyone's favorite web-slinger.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:15.760
When that didn't work out, JJJ decided that
it would probably a good idea to do some mad

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:18.970
science on him and give him a robotic scorpion
tail instead.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.920
This was not, in fact, a good idea.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:25.220
In 2005, however, Gargan decided to go in
a bit of a different direction.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:29.031
When Eddie Brock found Jesus, he decided to
give up the symbiote and auction it off to

00:05:29.031 --> 00:05:30.120
the highest bidder.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.300
Gargan wound up with it, became the new Venom,
and not only did he fight Spider-Man in this

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:38.320
identity, he actually replaced him by joining
up with Norman Osborn’s government-approved

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.860
team of Dark Avengers.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.630
As for Eddie, the combination of another villain's
healing powers and a chance encounter with

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:47.810
an old symbiote caused a reaction that gave
him his powers back — and more.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:51.780
It turned him into a sort of color-inverted
version of Venom who had the ability to cure

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.080
others of symbiote-related afflictions, and
he against fought crime as — wait for it

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.770
— Anti-Venom.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:59.440
Viva Venom variety

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:03.450
Eddie Brock and Mac Gargan are the most well-known
Venoms, but they’re not the only characters

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.020
to take on the identity.

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:08.910
In recent years, it’s bounced around the
Marvel Universe so much that it seems like

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:10.530
everyone’s had a shot — even Groot.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:14.680
Here’s a quick rundown: Flash Thompson,
who first appeared back in the ‘60s as the

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:18.960
high school bully who constantly picked on
"Puny Parker" before eventually becoming his

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:22.830
friend, lost his legs while serving in the
army in Iraq.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.900
In 2011, his heroic ideals and history of
valor in service made him the military's ideal

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:31.460
candidate for a program designed to create
a new kind of super-soldier: Agent Venom.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:35.970
He was bonded to the symbiote for 48 hours
at a time to keep him from losing control,

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:39.720
and served as a member of both the Secret
Avengers and the Guardians of the Galaxy before

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:45.110
losing the symbiote back to Eddie Brock and
becoming, wait for it again, Agent Anti-Venom.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.670
If that wasn’t weird enough, Thompson’s
time serving alongside the Guardians also

00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:53.290
involved a mishap that saw not just Groot
being taken over, but Rocket, too.

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:57.290
And if you’ve ever been startled by a raccoon
by your trash cans, just imagine how bad it

00:06:57.290 --> 00:07:00.820
would be if he had a laser gun and was also
bonded with a super-powered alien.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:02.520
It doesn’t end there, though.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:06.950
Over the years, the symbiote has briefly bonded
with Deadpool, Wolverine, Captain Marvel,

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:11.550
a villain named Lee Price, Drax the Destroyer,
Doctor Octopus when he was living in Peter

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:16.030
Parker’s body, another version of Wolverine,
and a kid who became Venom because he wanted

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:18.410
girls to write fan-fiction about him.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:19.630
No, really.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:22.030
And that’s just in the regular Marvel continuity.

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:26.010
If you expand it out to What If stories and
alternate universes, Venom’s been, well,

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:30.620
pretty much everyone from the Punisher to
Galactus, the devourer of worlds.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:33.240
But believe it or not, that’s not the weirdest
one.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.140
Beware the V-Rex

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:38.410
If you go into it looking to find out what
happened to your favorite superheroes, Mark

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:42.180
Millar and Steve McNiven's Old Man Logan is
a pretty huge bummer.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:46.720
Focused on an older Wolverine and a blinded
Hawkeye driving across a devastated America

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:50.820
that's been carved up by supervillains, it
devotes a lot of pages to revealing how the

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:54.210
heroes of the Marvel Universe died…or worse.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.000
If you like Venom, though, buckle up.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:59.780
As Wolverine and Hawkeye drive Spider-Man’s
old car through a chunk of the midwest that’s

00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:04.180
been taken over by the dinosaurs of the Savage
Land, they accidentally get on the wrong side

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:08.540
of a Tyrannosaurus Rex that's bonded with
the Venom symbiote to create Earth's perfect

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:09.740
killing machine.

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:11.500
Maybe next time, drive a car without its own
arch-enemy.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:12.900
Thanks for watching!

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.520
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.259
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

